\f0\b General comments
\f1\b0 \
\
Tables could be more polished. \

\f0\b We agree and apologize; the tables in the submitted version were rough and have been re-formatted, with improved captions.
\f1\b0 \
\
\
My only concern is the complexity of the study design, which is not balanced with inconsistently varying growth treatments (multiple marine and freshwater species grown at different temperatures, salinities, irradiances and growth medium). For instance, fresh water species were grown under salinities above their optimum (obviously), whereas marine species were mostly grown under salinities below their optimum. The only exception where a marine species was grown above their optimum salinity (also the only species grown at 700 mM NaCl) was also the only species where an effect of salinity on S-state cycling was found. \
\
Also, the cultures were grown under different growth irradiances, but it is not tested nor discussed whether this could affect results. Clearly, it is not always possible to grow different lineages under the same conditions and it is interesting to test if the results are consistent among these different treatments and lineages, but the reasoning for chosen treatments is mostly lacking and not all results are shown or discussed. I recommend a more thorough description of the reasoning behind the different study treatments and to clearly state associated objectives.\
\

\f0\b We agree. \
The salinity ranges were chosen based upon growth-permissive ranges for the Chlamydomonas strains, established in previous studies.\
We sought to test healthy cultures, so kept the salinities within well tolerated ranges.\
With these slow-growing psychrophiles, there was an element of 'experiment of opportunity' on the inclusion of strains x growth temperatures x growth lights.\
In the case of Fragilariopsis cylindricus we did have cultures grown at two different temperatures, with evidence for an effect of temperature now included.
\f1\b0 \

\f0\b Ideally growth light could/should be included in a future study as a possible influence on responses; we mentioned these issues in the revised version.
\f1\b0 \
\
\
Specific Comments:\
- What deserves more emphasis is the difference between polar and temperate species at the same growth temperature, since the conclusion and title suggest a direct comparison. It is clearly shown that the probability of non-radiative charge recombination increases with temperature, but is this a conserved trait or is it a specific adaptation of polar species? It is great that temperate and polar species were measured at least at one common temperature. This can disentangle whether the results are a direct effect of temperature or whether the temperate and polar species developed distinct adaptations to low light. Now this comparison is a bit buried in the individual species response to temperature, but the comparison could be made more clear by a separate figure making a direct comparison or at least a paragraph reporting these differences. \
\

\f0\b Thank you.\
We now emphasize  that for both diatoms and greens, polar and temperate strains achieve similar peak damping indices, but the polar strains maintain damping indices over wider ranges of conditions.  \
We think the polar strains show suppression of recombination at low temperatures, because they can grow and thrive at low temperatures, not because of some specific changes in their PSII.\
Although, Ivanov et al. have shown differences in the probabilities of recombinations (activation energies) across taxa and over acclimatory periods.
\f1\b0 \
\
- Throughout the text it is mainly reported that 32 successive single turnover flashes were used, but also sometimes 20-32. Why was the number not always 32 and in which cases was it a lower number? Could this have had an effect on the damping index? In figures 5 and 6 it seems the maximum damping index is 16, but it would be good to report on this.\
\

\f0\b We clarified the Materials & Methods as to the number of successive single turnover flashes.\
In most runs we used 32 successive flashes.\
During later runs we realized that, as the reviewer notes, the maximum damping index detected was <= 16 flashes.\
Because the runs at longer flash spacings are fairly time consuming (ex. 32 flashes x 8 s @ 256 s, and because our experimental design meant we ran different flash spacings on the same sample, we cut back the number of flashes in some later runs, if it was clear that oscillations had fully damped out.\
It was late, I am old and tired...\

\f1\b0 \
- The addition of salinity as a study treatment does not seem to be fully integrated with the rest of the study. It is argued that salinity affects PSI, cyclic electron flow and NPQ, but how is this expected to affect S-state cycling under extremely low light? Why are confounding effects expected? This is not included in the objectives framed at the end of the introduction, nor is salinity mentioned in the title, nor the abstract. \
\

\f0\b Co-author P. Osmers has a manuscript in review showing that salinity interacts with stability of PSII in the psychrophile Chlamydomonas strains.\
We had access to Chlamydomonas growing across ranges of tolerated salinities, so decided to include that axis in our analyses, as a potential interacting factor.\
As the reviewer notes, we detected no significant influence of changing salinity, except within *Chlamydomonas priscui*. \
We are not confident, yet, whether salinity is a potential general factor, although clearly extreme salinities would introduce destabilizing stresses.\
Given our limited analyses and findings on salinity, we omitted the factor from the title and abstract.\
\
We expanded our justifications for including salinity in the Introduction, and in the Discussion.\

\f1\b0 \
- If the suppression of energetically wasteful 'misses' in PSII is an adaptation to extremely low light, then I would at least expect a test between culture grown under different growth irradiances and a short discussion on why this did not (or does) have an effect.\
\

\f0\b It was not feasible for us to grow cultures at the extreme low lights equivalent to our accessible flash spacings.\
A future study could potentially grow cultures at moderately low light, then shift them down to test ability to grow  or maintain at extreme low light (0.02 umol photons m-2 s-1); but achieving such low lights is technically difficult in the lab.\
In contrast, field studies are currently underway to test the approach on natural populations under Arctic ice cover, at extremely low ambient light.
